# SYNTAX TEST "text.html.tiddlywiki5" "A code region reads literally until a label types it"
# TiddlyWiki hands a code span and a code block to no parser, and every shipped grammar reached for
# leaves them alone: VS Code's markdown, wooorm's markdown-tm-language, Pygments, nvim-treesitter,
# Helix and tree-sitter-markdown all decline to read inside an inline span, and none defaults an
# unlabelled fence to the host tongue. So neither reads as wikitext here.
#
# A label types it, and then it colours. The set follows the ecosystem's shape — a canonical name
# plus short aliases, with an extension among them the way js carries cjs and yaml carries yml.
# NEVER claim wikitext or wiki: the ecosystem owns both, and they mean MediaWiki.
#
# TiddlyWiki's own explicit typing is the $$$ block, which dispatches a real parser on its type.
A tick `<<amacro>>` reads literally.
#       ^^^^^^^^^^ markup.raw.inline.tiddlywiki5

```
<<amacro>> reads literally too.
#<- markup.inline.raw.block.tiddlywiki5
```

```tw
<<amacro>> colours under a label.
# ^^^^^^ variable.name.procedure.tiddlywiki5
```

```tid
<<amacro>> colours under the extension alias.
# ^^^^^^ variable.name.procedure.tiddlywiki5
```

```mem
<<amacro>> colours under the dialect label.
# ^^^^^^ variable.name.procedure.tiddlywiki5
```

$$$text/vnd.tiddlywiki
<<amacro>> colours where the parser itself dispatches.
# ^^^^^^ variable.name.procedure.tiddlywiki5
$$$

An unterminated `tick stands as prose.
#<- meta.paragraph.tiddlywiki5

An ordinary sentence stands after it.
#<- meta.paragraph.tiddlywiki5
